Original commit body from 63c4b9d94a0d0060e37c7ef1203ebe4fdc20021e:

Measured on two unmodified upstream repos by recomputing the gate over each
run's own cached survey: express reported 17 modules "the I/O catalog could
not classify" of which 7 were first-party and only 4 genuine; bellman reported
23 of which 6 were first-party. `qualifying_only` is `not unknown`, so ONE
noise entry withholds a verdict -- on both repos the first-party entries ALONE
were sufficient to withhold every claim, and the real dependencies were buried:
the rendered `details` truncates at five and express's opened with `..`, `../`,
`../../db`, making all four genuine ones invisible.

Now 17 -> 10 and 23 -> 18, opening on `assert, body-parser, document,
mime-types, node:http`.

THREE MECHANISMS, each rule exact rather than heuristic:

  1. rust `crate::` / `super::` / `self::` are path qualifiers resolving within
     the current crate, and all three are reserved words Cargo cannot publish
     under, so no dependency set can contain one.
  2. a javascript specifier beginning `./` or `../` is resolved against the
     importing file and never looked up in node_modules. RELATIVE only -- an
     ABSOLUTE path names a system header this analysis did not read.
  3. language-independent, and not named on the item: `_analyzed_modules`
     folded `/` into `.` on the SRC side while the callee side was compared
     RAW, so express's own `lib/utils` could never match its own analyzed
     `lib.utils`. The fold now has ONE home instead of three.

A REGISTRY PLUS A GATE, not a better literal. Every language the boundary gate
can reach declares a `FirstPartyModuleGrammar` WITH ITS BASIS -- an empty one
being a valid answer that has to be written down -- and a parity test drives
the denominator from the shipped catalogues. It FAILS OPEN, the opposite of
`analyzer_disclosure`'s direction, because suppressing on a rule nobody wrote
down is what would hide a real dependency.

THE FIRST CUT WAS REFUTED BY ITS OWN CONTROL RUN, ON CADDY, BEFORE SHIPPING.
Folding the separator and then running the existing prefix loop lets `os/exec`
shorten to `os` and match caddy's own `internal/filesystems/os.go`: 13 modules
suppressed, TEN of them Go standard library -- crypto/tls, crypto/x509,
os/exec and seven more -- because the repo owns a file called `crypto.go`.
`os/exec` IS the subprocess boundary. express and bellman looked clean under
that cut; only the Go repo showed it. The shipped rule tests the folded
spelling WHOLE and never shortens it.

NON-DESTRUCTION ASSERTED in the same runs: caddy unchanged (55/76/3 chains,
all violated), httpx unchanged (0 modules moved), NEWLY REPORTED 0 on all four.

NO VERDICT FLIPPED, and that is the point rather than a shortfall: what remains
is stdlib enumeration, and only python.yaml declares any `module_completeness`
while 14 of 15 catalogued languages declare zero (WI-lutuh). This makes that
work's output readable; it does not substitute for it.

RESIDUALS FILED RATHER THAN GLOSSED:
  INV-lakom (P1) the prefix-shortening hole is PRE-EXISTING for dotted
      languages and still reproduces -- a repo owning `myapp/os/helpers.py` and
      calling `os.path.join` reports coverage COMPLETE over stdlib `os`. This
      change declines to widen it; it does not close it.
  INV-vivok (P3) a repo referring to itself by its published package name is
